enclave socket level-check: kernel truth beats a stale readiness cache
======================================================================

THE DEFECT (the "tunnel stall" / engine freeze, risc-box 2026-08-20): under
load, a tenant's ENTIRE socket set intermittently freezes 0.5-14s on the
fleet (0.2-1.2s reproduced locally) while the guest's own event loop stays
healthy. The guest is a sync wasip2 app doing nonblocking reads/writes every
main-loop turn; the engine serves those from tokio's per-socket readiness
CACHE (`try_read`/`try_write`/`poll_*_ready` short-circuit on cached bits,
no syscall). The cache is refreshed only by edge-triggered epoll dispatch,
and an edge consumed at the wrong moment never re-fires: a socket that
STAYS writable/readable generates no new edges. Result: the engine tells
the guest "not ready" about a socket the kernel would serve immediately,
for as long as nothing else resyncs the cache.

MEASURED, instrumented locally (risc-box SET build, 1-CPU pin, 2 video
streams + 7/s status probe; ENCLAVE_SOCKDBG=1):
  - all active sockets went check_write=0 with state=Ready SIMULTANEOUSLY
    for up to 935ms, releasing at the same millisecond (one dispatch frees
    the set at once - exactly the fleet's whole-socket-set freeze shape);
  - a 10ms heartbeat task on the runtime logged ZERO gaps >100ms through
    every stall: the tokio workers and drivers were healthy and scheduled;
  - with the fix live, 2,098 write-side and 64 read-side divergences in 5
    minutes (kernel POLLOUT/bytes vs cache "not ready"), every one absorbed
    by the fallback: probes went 59 stalls/10min worst 1.2s -> 0 stalls,
    worst 14ms. The plain (non-SET) build shows the same defect at ~1/7th
    the rate - two SET guest threads just multiply the trigger interleavings.

THE FIX: on the three guest-facing paths (send, recv, readiness polls),
when the CACHED path says "not ready", ask the KERNEL before believing it -
a direct nonblocking send/recv, or a zero-timeout poll(2). The cache stays
the fast path; the syscall only happens where the old code stalled. Level
truth cannot lie, spurious readiness just costs one syscall, and both the
sync (noop-waker) and async (real-waker) callers keep their contracts: the
waker is always registered before the fallback answers Ready.

Observed oddity, recorded honestly: poll_write_ready(noop) reports Pending
while try_write microseconds later succeeds - tokio's two readiness views
disagree with EACH OTHER, not just with the kernel. The level check
sidesteps whichever internal inconsistency that is; upstreaming a tokio-
level diagnosis is follow-up work, this patch makes the fleet honest now.

Also carries ENCLAVE_SOCKDBG=1 (host process env, off by default, zero cost
when off): logs read/write divergences, >=200ms empty-read streaks with a
direct FIONREAD verdict, >=200ms check_write=0 streaks with the write-state
variant, and a 10ms runtime heartbeat that logs scheduling gaps >100ms -
the exact gauges that separated "driver starved" from "cache lied" without
needing ss/strace inside a CVM.

Touches crates/wasi/src/sockets/tcp.rs (mechanism + instrumentation),
crates/wasi/src/p2/tcp.rs (check_write instrumentation), sockets/mod.rs
(re-exports). No new dependencies: rustix event/net features were already
enabled for wasmtime-wasi. UDP and accept keep the old behavior (no stall
observed there yet); if a UDP-shaped stall ever shows, apply the same
pattern to sockets/udp.rs.
